A linked-list stack pushes at the head because adding a new node there is fast and takes O(1) time. If we push at the tail without a tail reference, we need to traverse the list to find the last node, which takes O(n) time. An array queue must be circular so it can reuse empty spaces at the beginning of the array after items are removed. The modulo operator allows the rear index to return to index 0 after reaching the end of the array. This prevents wasting available space and keeps queue operations efficient.